00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
well i'm joined now by shadow  environment secretary jim mcmahon a very  good morning to you so the union's  asking questions over labour's handling  of the strike so first of all was sekir  stama right to sack the shadow transport  minister  sam terry  i mean first of all the trade unions are  doing a very important job at  representing working people that are  really feeling the pinch after 12 years  of a tory government where the economy  is stagnated and people are really  feeling the squeeze now in the cost of  living crisis and so the trade unions uh  with that collective power of workers is  very important and i've been a member of  the trade union since i was 17 in my  first job i believe in that collective  power and i believe in workers coming  together what the labour party needs to  do is to look to the country and to  convince a country that we are to be  trusted with the nation's finances that  we are to be trusted with public  services and that we would to be trusted  for their families and that's about  convincing them that we're ready to  govern and so what the role that we're  playing is about holding the government  to account of course but it's also about  looking to the country and saying there  is an alternative to the tory uh flat  line in the economy there is a  alternative to the torah is completely  stripping out our public services and  there's an alternative to a government  frankly that is standing by during a  cost of living crisis that's really  hitting working people  well if the unions are as you say  good at representing working people why  is it such a bad thing for a labour  politician to to show their support on  the picket line  this is not about whether somebody was  on a picket line or not it was about as  i understand it the broadcast round uh  that was done with our authorization and  all of the assignments were collective  responsibility uh on the shadow cabinet  and that's very important and when that  isn't followed then the chief whip has  to take action to ensure that discipline  can be maintained because if we can't  organize ourselves in opposition then we  can't look to the country and say that  we're ready to govern and that's the  critical thing i do not want to go to  another general election to wake up on  that friday and find that the tour is a  won again we need to look to the country  and show them that there is an  alternative and when it comes to being  on the side of working people listen he  was the labour party the right at the  start of the year of the year was called  for a windfall tax on the oil and gas  giants it's a labour party that voted  down the national insurance increases  and it's the labour party that are  bringing forward plans to deal with the  cost of living crisis that people are  going through  but do you think the unions feel  supported by labour  i've been the trade unions from all the  relationships that i've had and you know  right throughout my uh political journey  we've always told shoulder shoulder with  our trade union colleagues they're an  important part of the labour family and  we have different roles to play in this  the trade unions of course has to  organize and make sure that workers have  that collective voice when they don't  feel that they're being listened to but  i look to the conservatives and say well  where are they where is branch at on the  rail strike for instance when he's  completely missing in action instead of  making it better he's making it worse by  fanning the flames and i think the  government will need to step up now and  recognize that the country is grinding  to a whole under their leadership and if  they don't show leadership we are going  to head into the summer and into the  winter with further industrial action  and further cost of living crisis  hitting households  well yes and we talked to dave ward of  the communication workers union earlier  you talk of other industrial action he  said he was talking to other unions  about forms of collective action what do  you make of that do you support some  kind of collective action and what would  that look like  it feels to me as all the conservative  government is right in that to be honest  through their actions through their  words  the way that they are tackling the cost  of living crisis the way they haven't  got a plan to control inflation and  critically the way they haven't got a  plan to grow the economy i think all of  us now have looked at the last 12 years  at the stagnation in the economy where  we haven't seen the growth that really  delivers a quality of life for working  people where people are seeing their  wages stagnate and people don't think  that they're able to get on that  generational improvement that was a  british promise that if you work hard  and roll your sleeves up you can get on  in life has been denied people for the  last 12 years and people have absolutely  reached the end of their tether and it's  for the labour party to offer an  alternative which is what we're doing  our plan for government will see a fresh  start for britain uh changing the person  at the top of the conservative party  will not deliver the change his country  requires and it's very important that we  keep focused on that but it's for the  government of the day the conservative  party to deal with the cost of living  crisis to make sure that people can  afford to put food on the table because  if they don't because they are going to  naturally want the pay increase okay and  i want to ask you about the cost of  living in just a moment but what about  this issue of collective action we had  john mcdonnell the former shadow  chancellor on this program yesterday he  was saying that he would back a general  strike or coordinated action so would  you back one too  i want to see strike action avoided at  all costs i don't think anybody wants to  go on a picket line when they want to go  and do a day's work but people are  forced to do that when the negotiations  reach a position  a blockage  and so the question then is well what  can the government do to remove that  blockage to get the trade unions around  the table with the employer to try and  negotiate a solution that works for  working people and when the government  don't do that but even worse make it  much harder on the sidelines when  they're fanning the flames of dispute  doesn't help anybody so i won't see all  strike action avoided completely because  that's not what working people want  there's not a single person on that  picket line that wouldn't rather be in  work but they cannot continue to feel  the cost of the cost of living crisis  where things are getting worse and not  better so the government do need to  convene people they do need to find a  way through that and they need to bring  inflation under control to make sure  that people aren't feeling the squeeze  in the way that they are  how do you keep inflation under control  if you're paying higher wages  but that isn't what caused inflation  that we're seeing today these calls for  higher wages are as a result of the cost  of living crisis they are not the cause  of the cost of living crisis uh that we  see today listen my brief covers uh food  uh and drink which is essential for  people and we talk about gas and  electric bills we talk about council tax  national insurance and the rest but i do  think people feel the price of food  increase acutely when they go to the  supermarket and they're trying to  balance the family finances and  increasingly people can't buy the things  that they used to buy people are  skipping meals and actually what i  notice in my local supermarket is that  the food bank donation basket on the  other side of the till is far emptier  than it was before because people  haven't got their head room to be able  to donate even to the local food bank  what could the government have done they  could have taken action on the  fertilizer crisis they could have taken  action on the co2 crisis they could have  taken action on the labor crisis where  we have labor shortages which means that  people are going hungry on one hand but  there is literally food rotting in the  fields on the other there are forty  thousand pigs being called because we  haven't got the butchers in this country  and the cost of everything is going up  for producers as well as retailers and  consumers  well yes and energy bills are going up  the prediction is they'll go up even  higher than we had thought so what needs  to be done about it what's labour's plan  of action  well we need our short-term plan which  is the windfall tax on oil and gas  giants we need not to make it worse than  it is and that's why we voted against  the national insurance increase that's  really hitting families at a time when  they need help and not more taxes going  out of their wage slip but we also need  a long-term plan listen there are too  many homes in this country that are not  insulated that means that people are  paying far more for energy than they  otherwise would do because the  government have completely fallen back  uh on the the progress that was being  made under the previous labor government  to make sure that homes are of a decent  standard  if we're able to insulate homes we'll  save people hundreds of pounds a year on  energy we can reduce the amount of gas  that we use by 15  that reduces our dependence on  russian gas for instance but then also a  really ambitious plan to make sure that  we have new nuclear new on and offshore  alternative fuel as well to make sure  that we have that long-term plan because  we are in this crisis for a reason and  the reason is that we have not invested  in our communities we have not invested  in energy and the benefit of course  isn't just that we have less  bills to pay in terms of gas and  electric it's creating those decent  working-class jobs in every one of our  regions so britain can grow and thrive  together  jim mcmahon we appreciate your time this  morning thanks very much indeed for  joining us

